export const SKILL_FILES: Record<string, string> = {
  "SKILL.md": "---\nname: vastitecture-modeling\ndescription: Create and change architecture models in a Vastitecture workspace (C4, ArchiMate, DDD and TOGAF in one model) by writing a name-based model spec and applying it with the vastitecture CLI. Use when the user asks to model, document, diagram or map a system, application landscape, capabilities, bounded contexts, a migration or decision records, or when a folder contains vastitecture.yaml.\n---\n\n# Vastitecture modelling\n\nVastitecture keeps one architecture model and shows it in several notations: a system is one element\nthat can appear in a C4 container view, an ArchiMate application view and a DDD context map. A\nworkspace is a folder of YAML files (`vastitecture.yaml`, `model/`, `views/`, `adrs/`) kept in Git.\n\n**Do not write those YAML files by hand.** They carry generated ids and diagram coordinates. Write a\nshort **model spec** that names things and let `vastitecture apply` validate it, merge it into the\nmodel and lay the views out.\n\n## 1. Find the command\n\nUse the first that works:\n\n1. `vastitecture` on the PATH.\n2. `node <repo>/packages/cli/dist/vastitecture.mjs` inside a Vastitecture checkout (build it first with\n   `pnpm --filter @vastitecture/cli build` if `dist/` is missing).\n3. `node vastitecture.mjs`, after downloading `vastitecture.mjs` from the latest GitHub release of\n   Vastitecture. It is one file and runs on Node 20 or later.\n\nIf the tool offers MCP tools named `list_types`, `show_model`, `apply_model_spec` and `check_model`,\nuse those instead: they are the same commands, served by `vastitecture mcp`.\n\nBelow, `vastitecture` stands for whichever you found.\n\n## 2. Workflow\n\n1. **Workspace.** If there is no `vastitecture.yaml`, create one: `vastitecture new <folder>` (blank), or\n   `vastitecture new <folder> --sample banking` to start from an industry sample (`vastitecture samples`\n   lists them).\n2. **Read before you write.** Run `vastitecture show <folder>` to see what exists. Reuse existing names\n   exactly. An element with the same name is updated, not duplicated.\n3. **Look up the vocabulary.** Run `vastitecture types` (or `--notation c4`, or `--format json`). Use\n   only the ids it lists. The full list is in [reference/notations.md](reference/notations.md).\n4. **Write a spec** in a YAML file. The format is in [reference/spec-format.md](reference/spec-format.md),\n   and there are complete examples in [examples/](examples/).\n5. **Dry run:** `vastitecture apply <folder> spec.yaml --dry-run`. If it reports problems, fix the spec.\n   Every error names the valid choices; nothing is written while there are errors.\n6. **Apply:** `vastitecture apply <folder> spec.yaml`. Apply is idempotent: running the same spec again\n   changes nothing.\n7. **Check:** `vastitecture check <folder>`. Resolve errors; mention warnings to the user.\n8. **Tell the user** what you added. They can open the folder in the Vastitecture app, or export a view,\n   for example `vastitecture export <folder> --format mermaid-c4 --view \"<view name>\"`.\n\n## 3. A minimal spec\n\n```yaml\nelements:\n  - name: Customer\n    type: c4:Person\n    description: Buys from the shop.\n  - name: Online Shop\n    type: c4:SoftwareSystem\n    description: Lets customers browse and order.\n    owner: Team Checkout\n    lifecycle: { phase: active }\n  - name: Web App\n    type: c4:Container\n    parent: Online Shop\n    properties: { technology: React }\n  - name: Payment Provider\n    type: c4:SoftwareSystem\n    properties: { location: External }\nrelationships:\n  - { from: Customer, to: Web App, type: c4:Uses, label: Orders with, technology: HTTPS }\n  - { from: Web App, to: Payment Provider, type: c4:Uses, label: Takes payment via }\nviews:\n  - name: Online Shop – containers\n    viewpoint: c4:container\n    subject: Online Shop\n    rows: [[Customer], [Online Shop, Web App], [Payment Provider]]\n```\n\n## 4. Modelling rules that matter\n\n- **One element, many views.** Model a system once and add it to C4, ArchiMate and DDD views. Do not\n  create \"Online Shop (ArchiMate)\" next to \"Online Shop\".\n- **Pick the notation by question:**\n  - C4 for \"what are the systems and containers and how do they talk\";\n  - ArchiMate for capabilities, business processes, value streams, motivation (drivers, goals,\n    requirements) and layered views;\n  - DDD for domains, subdomains, bounded contexts, team ownership, context-map patterns and event\n    storms;\n  - TOGAF for building blocks and ADM phases.\n\n  See [reference/modeling-guide.md](reference/modeling-guide.md).\n- **Nesting** uses `parent`. A container's parent is its software system; a sub-capability's parent is\n  its capability. In a view, a child is drawn inside its parent when both are listed.\n- **Relationships read as sentences**, from the dependent to the dependency: \"Web App → Orders DB:\n  Reads and writes\". Give `technology` for protocols.\n- **Views stay small:** 5 to 15 elements. Use `rows` for a readable top-to-bottom order: people at the\n  top, the subject in the middle, external systems at the bottom.\n- **Portfolio facts go in `owner`, `lifecycle` and `properties`:**\n  - `criticality`, `technicalFit`, `functionalFit`, `annualCost` and `monthlyCost`;\n  - `dataClassification` (public, internal, confidential, restricted);\n  - `location: External` for third parties in C4, and `external: true` for data-flow analysis.\n\n  The app's fact sheets, heat maps, roadmaps and Insights read them.\n- **Decisions:** record significant choices as `decisions` (architecture decision records) linked to\n  the elements they affect.\n- **Do not invent facts.** If the user has not said who owns something or what it costs, leave it out\n  or ask.\n\n## 5. Changing and removing\n\n- Update: list the element by its existing name with only the fields to change. `properties` are merged;\n  set a property to `null` to remove it.\n- Rename: give the element's `id` (from `vastitecture show --format json` or the files in `model/`)\n  with the new `name`. Its relationships, views and decisions follow.\n- Duplicate names: when two elements share a name (a \"Payments\" system and a \"Payments\"\n  capability), `show` prints their ids. Refer to them by id in `from`, `to`, `parent` and view lists.\n- Remove: `remove: { elements: [...], relationships: [{ from, to, type }], views: [...], decisions: [...] }`.\n  Removing an element also removes its relationships and takes it out of views.\n- Re-listing a view with the same elements keeps its layout. Listing different elements lays the view\n  out again.\n",
  "examples/archimate-capabilities.yaml": "# Capabilities with fit and cost (for the heat map), the systems that realise them, and the\n# regulation behind a requirement. Apply after c4-system.yaml: it reuses \"Online Shop\".\nelements:\n  - { name: Sales, type: archimate:Capability, description: Sell to customers., properties: { functionalFit: Adequate } }\n  - { name: Order Management, type: archimate:Capability, parent: Sales, description: Take and track orders., properties: { functionalFit: Excellent, annualCost: 420000 } }\n  - { name: Payments, type: archimate:Capability, parent: Sales, description: Take money safely., properties: { functionalFit: Excellent, annualCost: 90000 } }\n  - { name: Card security rules, type: archimate:Driver, description: Card industry security standard (PCI DSS). }\n  - { name: No card numbers stored, type: archimate:Requirement, description: Only the payment provider holds card numbers. }\nrelationships:\n  - { from: Online Shop, to: Order Management, type: archimate:Realization }\n  - { from: Payment Provider, to: Payments, type: archimate:Realization }\n  - { from: Card security rules, to: No card numbers stored, type: archimate:Influence }\n  - { from: Payment Provider, to: No card numbers stored, type: archimate:Realization }\nviews:\n  - name: Capability map\n    viewpoint: archimate:capability-map\n    rows: [[Sales, Order Management, Payments]]\n  - name: Regulation\n    viewpoint: archimate:motivation\n    rows: [[Card security rules], [No card numbers stored]]\n  - name: How requirements are met\n    viewpoint: archimate:requirements-realization\n    rows: [[No card numbers stored], [Payment Provider]]\n",
  "examples/c4-system.yaml": "# A system, its containers and its neighbours, in C4. Apply to a blank workspace:\n#   vastitecture new shop && vastitecture apply shop c4-system.yaml\nelements:\n  - name: Customer\n    type: c4:Person\n    description: Buys from the shop.\n  - name: Online Shop\n    type: c4:SoftwareSystem\n    description: Lets customers browse the catalogue, place orders and pay.\n    owner: Team Checkout\n    tags: [customer-facing]\n    lifecycle: { phase: active, dates: { active: \"2021-03-01\" } }\n    properties: { criticality: Mission critical, technicalFit: Adequate, annualCost: 420000, dataClassification: confidential }\n  - { name: Web App, type: c4:Container, parent: Online Shop, description: Storefront in the browser., properties: { technology: React } }\n  - { name: Order API, type: c4:Container, parent: Online Shop, description: Accepts orders and takes payment., properties: { technology: Node.js } }\n  - { name: Orders DB, type: c4:Container, parent: Online Shop, description: Orders and payment state., properties: { technology: PostgreSQL, kind: Database } }\n  - name: Payment Provider\n    type: c4:SoftwareSystem\n    description: Authorises card payments.\n    properties: { location: External, external: true }\nrelationships:\n  - { from: Customer, to: Web App, type: c4:Uses, label: Browses and orders with, technology: HTTPS }\n  - { from: Web App, to: Order API, type: c4:Uses, label: Places orders via, technology: JSON/HTTPS }\n  - { from: Order API, to: Orders DB, type: c4:Uses, label: Reads and writes, technology: SQL }\n  - { from: Order API, to: Payment Provider, type: c4:Uses, label: Takes payment via, technology: HTTPS }\nviews:\n  - name: Online Shop – context\n    viewpoint: c4:context\n    subject: Online Shop\n    rows: [[Customer], [Online Shop], [Payment Provider]]\n  - name: Online Shop – containers\n    viewpoint: c4:container\n    subject: Online Shop\n    rows: [[Customer], [Online Shop, Web App, Order API, Orders DB], [Payment Provider]]\n",
  "examples/ddd-context-map.yaml": "# Subdomains, team ownership, a context map and an event storm. Apply after c4-system.yaml.\nelements:\n  - { name: E-commerce, type: ddd:Domain, description: Selling goods online. }\n  - { name: Ordering, type: ddd:Subdomain, parent: E-commerce, description: Fast, reliable checkout., properties: { subdomainType: Core } }\n  - { name: Team Checkout, type: ddd:Team, description: Owns ordering. }\n  - name: Online Shop\n    properties:\n      purpose: Take an order from basket to confirmed payment.\n      domainClassification: Core\n      ubiquitousLanguage: \"Basket: items a customer intends to buy.\\nOrder: a basket the customer committed to.\"\n  - { name: Place order, type: ddd:Command, parent: Online Shop, description: A customer submits their basket. }\n  - { name: Order, type: ddd:Aggregate, parent: Online Shop, description: Keeps an order consistent. }\n  - { name: Order placed, type: ddd:DomainEvent, parent: Online Shop, description: An order was accepted. }\n  - { name: Charge when placed, type: ddd:Policy, parent: Online Shop, description: Whenever an order is placed, take payment. }\nrelationships:\n  - { from: Online Shop, to: Ordering, type: ddd:Implements }\n  - { from: Team Checkout, to: Online Shop, type: ddd:Owns }\n  - { from: Payment Provider, to: Online Shop, type: ddd:AnticorruptionLayer, label: payment results, properties: { downstreamRoles: ACL } }\n  - { from: Place order, to: Order, type: ddd:Triggers }\n  - { from: Order, to: Order placed, type: ddd:Triggers }\n  - { from: Order placed, to: Charge when placed, type: ddd:Triggers }\nviews:\n  - name: Context map\n    viewpoint: ddd:context-map\n    rows: [[Team Checkout], [Payment Provider, Online Shop]]\n  - name: Place an order (event storm)\n    viewpoint: ddd:event-storming\n    rows: [[Place order, Order, Order placed, Charge when placed]]\n  - name: Online Shop – canvas\n    viewpoint: ddd:canvas\n    subject: Online Shop\ndecisions:\n  - title: Keep card data with the payment provider\n    status: accepted\n    date: \"2026-01-10\"\n    elements: [Payment Provider, Order API]\n    body: |\n      ## Context\n      Storing card numbers puts every system that touches them in PCI DSS scope.\n\n      ## Decision\n      The payment provider holds card numbers; we keep tokens.\n\n      ## Consequences\n      Less compliance scope; refunds go through the provider's API.\n",
  "reference/modeling-guide.md": "# Modelling guide\n\nHow to turn a description of a system or an organisation into a good Vastitecture model.\n\n## Start from the question\n\n| The user wants to know | Model | Views |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| What systems exist and who uses them | C4 people and software systems | `c4:landscape`, `c4:context` |\n| What a system is made of | C4 containers (apps, services, databases, queues) under the system | `c4:container` |\n| What a container is made of | C4 components under the container | `c4:component` |\n| Where it runs | `c4:DeploymentNode` and `c4:InfrastructureNode`, nested | `c4:deployment` |\n| What the business can do, and how well | ArchiMate capabilities in a hierarchy, with `functionalFit` and `annualCost`; systems realise them | `archimate:capability-map` |\n| How work flows | ArchiMate processes linked by `archimate:Triggering`, served by systems | `archimate:business-process-cooperation` |\n| How value is delivered end to end | An `archimate:ValueStream` composed of stage value streams linked by `archimate:Flow`, served by capabilities | `archimate:value-stream` |\n| Why: regulation, goals, requirements | `archimate:Driver` → `archimate:Goal` / `archimate:Requirement` (Influence); systems realise requirements | `archimate:motivation` (drivers, goals, requirements only), `archimate:requirements-realization` (requirements with the systems) |\n| How systems cooperate | Systems with C4 dependencies shown on an ArchiMate view | `archimate:application` with `alsoShow: [c4]` |\n| Domain boundaries and team ownership | `ddd:Domain` → `ddd:Subdomain` (Core, Supporting, Generic); systems implement subdomains; teams own systems; context-map patterns between systems | `ddd:context-map` |\n| A business flow in detail | Event-storm stickies (`ddd:Command`, `ddd:Aggregate`, `ddd:DomainEvent`, `ddd:Policy`, `ddd:ReadModel`) as children of the system, linked by `ddd:Triggers` | `ddd:event-storming` |\n| What one bounded context is responsible for | Canvas properties on the system | `ddd:canvas` with `subject` |\n| Target architecture building blocks | `togaf:ArchitectureBuildingBlock` realised by `togaf:SolutionBuildingBlock`, which uses systems | `togaf:building-blocks` |\n| A migration (as-is to to-be) | Lifecycle dates: the old system `phaseOut` → `endOfLife`; the new one `plan` → `phaseIn` → `active` | Insights page and roadmap in the app; a landscape showing both |\n\n## Naming\n\n- Use the names people use: \"Payments Hub\", not \"payments-hub-svc\". Containers are named by role:\n  \"Order API\", \"Orders DB\".\n- Names must be unique enough to refer to. If two things share a name, qualify one: \"Payments\n  (subdomain)\" for the DDD subdomain beside the \"Payments\" capability.\n- Relationship labels are verb phrases read from `from` to `to`: \"Publishes OrderPlaced to\",\n  \"Authenticates customers with\".\n\n## How much to model\n\n- Prefer a correct small model over a large guessed one. Ask the user for missing facts that matter:\n  owners, which system is the system of record, and what is being replaced.\n- Each view should answer one question and hold 5 to 15 elements. Make another view rather than a\n  crowded one.\n- Model external parties (vendors, partners, regulators) as software systems with\n  `location: External` and `external: true`. They appear grey in C4, and the data-flow insight treats\n  them as outside the organisation.\n\n## Building up a model in steps\n\n1. A `c4:landscape` with the people and systems.\n2. A `c4:container` view for the system the user cares most about.\n3. A capability map, with systems realising capabilities.\n4. Context-map relationships and an event storm for the core flow.\n5. Lifecycle dates, owners and costs, so the app's portfolio pages and Insights have data.\n6. Decisions for the choices the user tells you about.\n\nApply after each step and run `vastitecture check`. Smaller specs give clearer errors.\n\n## Checking your work\n\n- `vastitecture check <folder>` validates types, viewpoint membership, relationship validity (where the\n  notation defines it) and the workspace's own rules.\n- A warning `not-in-viewpoint` means an element type does not belong in that viewpoint. Use the\n  suggested viewpoint or drop the element from the view.\n- `vastitecture export <folder> --format mermaid-c4 --view \"<name>\"` gives a quick text rendering to\n  show the user in chat.\n",
  "reference/notations.md": "# Notation vocabulary\n\nGenerated from the app's notation packs by `vastitecture types --format markdown`. Use these ids in model specs.\n\n## ArchiMate 4 (`archimate`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `archimate:Role` | Role | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Collaboration` | Collaboration | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Path` | Path | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Process` | Process | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Function` | Function | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Service` | Service | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Event` | Event | Common; properties: domain (Business \\| Application \\| Technology \\| Implementation) |\n| `archimate:Grouping` | Grouping | Common; can contain other elements |\n| `archimate:Location` | Location | Common |\n| `archimate:Stakeholder` | Stakeholder | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Driver` | Driver | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Assessment` | Assessment | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Goal` | Goal | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Outcome` | Outcome | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Principle` | Principle | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Requirement` | Requirement | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Meaning` | Meaning | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Value` | Value | Motivation |\n| `archimate:Resource` | Resource | Strategy |\n| `archimate:Capability` | Capability | Strategy |\n| `archimate:ValueStream` | Value Stream | Strategy |\n| `archimate:CourseOfAction` | Course Of Action | Strategy |\n| `archimate:BusinessActor` | Business Actor | Business |\n| `archimate:BusinessInterface` | Business Interface | Business |\n| `archimate:BusinessObject` | Business Object | Business |\n| `archimate:Product` | Product | Business |\n| `archimate:ApplicationComponent` | Application Component | Application |\n| `archimate:ApplicationInterface` | Application Interface | Application |\n| `archimate:DataObject` | Data Object | Application |\n| `archimate:Node` | Node | Technology |\n| `archimate:Device` | Device | Technology |\n| `archimate:SystemSoftware` | System Software | Technology |\n| `archimate:TechnologyInterface` | Technology Interface | Technology |\n| `archimate:CommunicationNetwork` | Communication Network | Technology |\n| `archimate:Artifact` | Artifact | Technology |\n| `archimate:Equipment` | Equipment | Technology |\n| `archimate:Facility` | Facility | Technology |\n| `archimate:DistributionNetwork` | Distribution Network | Technology |\n| `archimate:Material` | Material | Technology |\n| `archimate:WorkPackage` | Work Package | Implementation |\n| `archimate:Deliverable` | Deliverable | Implementation |\n| `archimate:Plateau` | Plateau | Implementation |\n| `archimate:Junction` | Junction | Common; properties: junctionType (and \\| or) |\n\n### Relationship types\n\n`archimate:Composition` (Composition), `archimate:Aggregation` (Aggregation), `archimate:Assignment` (Assignment), `archimate:Realization` (Realization), `archimate:Serving` (Serving), `archimate:Access` (Access), `archimate:Influence` (Influence), `archimate:Association` (Association), `archimate:Triggering` (Triggering), `archimate:Flow` (Flow), `archimate:Specialization` (Specialization)\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `archimate:organization` | Organization | `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:business-process-cooperation` | Business Process Cooperation | `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Product`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:product` | Product | `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Product`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Value`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:application-cooperation` | Application Cooperation | `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:application-usage` | Application Usage | `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:implementation-deployment` | Implementation and Deployment | `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:TechnologyInterface`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:Path`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:technology` | Technology | `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:TechnologyInterface`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:Path`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:technology-usage` | Technology Usage | `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:TechnologyInterface`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:Path`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:information-structure` | Information Structure | `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Meaning`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:service-realization` | Service Realization | `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Product`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Collaboration`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Event`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:physical` | Physical | `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:Path`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:layered` | Layered | any of this notation |\n| `archimate:stakeholder` | Stakeholder | `archimate:Stakeholder`, `archimate:Driver`, `archimate:Assessment`, `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:goal-realization` | Goal Realization | `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Principle`, `archimate:Requirement`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:requirements-realization` | Requirements Realization | `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Principle`, `archimate:Requirement`, `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Product`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:TechnologyInterface`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:motivation` | Motivation | `archimate:Stakeholder`, `archimate:Driver`, `archimate:Assessment`, `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Principle`, `archimate:Requirement`, `archimate:Meaning`, `archimate:Value`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:strategy` | Strategy | `archimate:Resource`, `archimate:Capability`, `archimate:ValueStream`, `archimate:CourseOfAction`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:capability-map` | Capability Map | `archimate:Capability`, `archimate:Resource`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:outcome-realization` | Outcome Realization | `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Capability`, `archimate:Resource`, `archimate:ValueStream`, `archimate:CourseOfAction`, `archimate:Value`, `archimate:Meaning`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:resource-map` | Resource Map | `archimate:Resource`, `archimate:Capability`, `archimate:WorkPackage`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:value-stream` | Value Stream | `archimate:ValueStream`, `archimate:Capability`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Stakeholder`, `archimate:Value`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:project` | Project | `archimate:WorkPackage`, `archimate:Deliverable`, `archimate:Plateau`, `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:migration` | Migration | `archimate:Plateau`, `archimate:Assessment`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:implementation-migration` | Implementation and Migration | `archimate:WorkPackage`, `archimate:Deliverable`, `archimate:Plateau`, `archimate:Stakeholder`, `archimate:Driver`, `archimate:Assessment`, `archimate:Goal`, `archimate:Outcome`, `archimate:Principle`, `archimate:Requirement`, `archimate:Meaning`, `archimate:Value`, `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:BusinessInterface`, `archimate:BusinessObject`, `archimate:Product`, `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Device`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:TechnologyInterface`, `archimate:CommunicationNetwork`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Equipment`, `archimate:Facility`, `archimate:DistributionNetwork`, `archimate:Material`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Location`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n| `archimate:application` | Application (cross-notation) | `archimate:ApplicationComponent`, `archimate:ApplicationInterface`, `archimate:DataObject`, `archimate:BusinessActor`, `archimate:Role`, `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Function`, `archimate:Process`, `archimate:SystemSoftware`, `archimate:Node`, `archimate:Artifact`, `archimate:Grouping`, `archimate:Junction` |\n\n## C4 model (`c4`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `c4:Person` | Person | Static; properties: location (Internal \\| External) |\n| `c4:SoftwareSystem` | Software System | Static; can contain other elements; properties: location (Internal \\| External) |\n| `c4:Container` | Container | Static; can contain other elements; properties: technology, kind (Application \\| Database \\| Queue \\| Browser \\| Mobile \\| Folder) |\n| `c4:Component` | Component | Static; properties: technology |\n| `c4:DeploymentNode` | Deployment Node | Deployment; can contain other elements; properties: technology, environment, instances |\n| `c4:InfrastructureNode` | Infrastructure Node | Deployment; properties: technology |\n\n### Relationship types\n\n`c4:Uses` (Uses)\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `c4:landscape` | System Landscape | `c4:Person`, `c4:SoftwareSystem` |\n| `c4:context` | System Context | `c4:Person`, `c4:SoftwareSystem` |\n| `c4:container` | Container | `c4:Person`, `c4:SoftwareSystem`, `c4:Container` |\n| `c4:component` | Component | `c4:Person`, `c4:SoftwareSystem`, `c4:Container`, `c4:Component` |\n| `c4:dynamic` | Dynamic | `c4:Person`, `c4:SoftwareSystem`, `c4:Container`, `c4:Component` |\n| `c4:deployment` | Deployment | `c4:DeploymentNode`, `c4:InfrastructureNode`, `c4:SoftwareSystem`, `c4:Container` |\n\n## Domain-Driven Design (`ddd`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ddd:Domain` | Domain | Strategic; can contain other elements |\n| `ddd:Subdomain` | Subdomain | Strategic; can contain other elements; properties: subdomainType (Core \\| Supporting \\| Generic) |\n| `ddd:BoundedContext` | Bounded Context | Strategic; properties: contextType (Feature \\| Application \\| System \\| Team), implementationTechnology, bigBallOfMud |\n| `ddd:ExternalSystem` | External System | Strategic |\n| `ddd:Team` | Team | Strategic |\n| `ddd:Module` | Module | Tactical; can contain other elements |\n| `ddd:Aggregate` | Aggregate | Tactical |\n| `ddd:Entity` | Entity | Tactical |\n| `ddd:ValueObject` | Value Object | Tactical |\n| `ddd:DomainEvent` | Domain Event | Tactical |\n| `ddd:Command` | Command | Tactical |\n| `ddd:Policy` | Policy | Tactical |\n| `ddd:ReadModel` | Read Model | Tactical |\n\n### Relationship types\n\n`ddd:Partnership` (Partnership), `ddd:SharedKernel` (Shared Kernel), `ddd:CustomerSupplier` (Customer/Supplier), `ddd:UpstreamDownstream` (Upstream/Downstream), `ddd:Conformist` (Conformist), `ddd:AnticorruptionLayer` (Anticorruption Layer), `ddd:OpenHostService` (Open Host Service), `ddd:PublishedLanguage` (Published Language), `ddd:SeparateWays` (Separate Ways), `ddd:BigBallOfMud` (Big Ball of Mud), `ddd:Implements` (Implements), `ddd:Owns` (Owns), `ddd:Triggers` (Triggers), `ddd:References` (References)\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ddd:context-map` | Context Map | `ddd:BoundedContext`, `ddd:ExternalSystem`, `ddd:Team`, `ddd:Domain`, `ddd:Subdomain` |\n| `ddd:canvas` | Bounded Context Canvas (form) |  |\n| `ddd:event-storming` | Event Storming | `ddd:DomainEvent`, `ddd:Command`, `ddd:Policy`, `ddd:ReadModel`, `ddd:Aggregate`, `ddd:ExternalSystem`, `ddd:Team` |\n| `ddd:tactical` | Tactical model | `ddd:BoundedContext`, `ddd:Module`, `ddd:Aggregate`, `ddd:Entity`, `ddd:ValueObject`, `ddd:DomainEvent`, `ddd:Command`, `ddd:Policy`, `ddd:ReadModel` |\n\n## TOGAF (`togaf`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `togaf:ArchitectureBuildingBlock` | Architecture Building Block | Building blocks |\n| `togaf:SolutionBuildingBlock` | Solution Building Block | Building blocks |\n\n### Relationship types\n\n`togaf:Realizes` (Realizes), `togaf:Uses` (Uses)\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `togaf:building-blocks` | Building blocks | any of this notation |\n\nProperties for elements of any notation: admPhase (Preliminary | A: Architecture Vision | B: Business Architecture | C: Information Systems Architectures | D: Technology Architecture | E: Opportunities and Solutions | F: Migration Planning | G: Implementation Governance | H: Architecture Change Management | Requirements Management), buildingBlock (Architecture (ABB) | Solution (SBB))\n\n## Generic (`generic`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `generic:Box` | Box | Shapes |\n| `generic:RoundedBox` | Rounded Box | Shapes |\n| `generic:Group` | Group | Shapes; can contain other elements |\n| `generic:Note` | Note | Shapes |\n| `generic:Image` | Image | Shapes; properties: href |\n| `generic:StartEnd` | Start / End | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Process` | Process | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Decision` | Decision | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Data` | Data | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Document` | Document | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Database` | Database | Flowchart |\n| `generic:Actor` | Actor | Flowchart |\n\n### Relationship types\n\n`generic:Arrow` (Arrow), `generic:Dashed` (Dashed arrow), `generic:Line` (Line)\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `generic:whiteboard` | Whiteboard | any of this notation |\n| `generic:flowchart` | Flowchart | any of this notation |\n\n## Inventory (`inventory`)\n\n### Element types\n\n| Id | Name | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n\n### Relationship types\n\nnone\n\n### Viewpoints\n\n| Id | Name | Element types allowed |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n\nProperties for elements of any notation: criticality (Mission critical | Business critical | Business operational | Administrative), technicalFit (Excellent | Adequate | Insufficient | Unreasonable), functionalFit (Excellent | Adequate | Insufficient | Unreasonable), annualCost\n\n",
  "reference/spec-format.md": "# Model spec format\n\nA model spec is a YAML (or JSON) document with any of these top-level keys. Every key is optional.\n`vastitecture apply` merges it into the workspace, and `vastitecture show` prints a workspace in the\nsame format, so you can read, edit and re-apply.\n\n```yaml\nworkspace: { name: \"Acme architecture\" }\nelements: [...]\nrelationships: [...]\nviews: [...]\ndecisions: [...]\nrules: [...]\nremove: { elements: [...], relationships: [...], views: [...], decisions: [...] }\n```\n\n## elements\n\n| Key | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `name` | yes | The element's name. It is how the rest of the spec refers to it. An existing element with this name is updated. |\n| `type` | for new elements | A type id from `vastitecture types`, for example `c4:SoftwareSystem` or `archimate:Capability`. |\n| `id` | no | Chooses between existing elements that share a name (`show` prints ids when names clash). With a different `name` it renames the element. For a new element it fixes the id. Anywhere an element name is expected you may give an id instead. |\n| `description` | no | One or two sentences. |\n| `parent` | no | Name of the containing element. Use `null` to clear it. |\n| `owner` | no | Team or person accountable. |\n| `tags` | no | List of strings. Replaces the existing tags. |\n| `lifecycle` | no | `{ phase, dates }`. `phase` is one of `plan`, `phaseIn`, `active`, `phaseOut` or `endOfLife`. `dates` maps a phase to `YYYY-MM-DD`; the dates drive roadmaps and the as-is / to-be comparison. |\n| `properties` | no | Map of key to value (text, number, boolean or list of text). Merged into existing properties; `null` deletes a key. |\n| `links` | no | `[{ kind: repo\\|runbook\\|adr\\|doc\\|other, href, label }]`. |\n\nUseful properties:\n- **Any element:**\n  - `criticality` (Mission critical, Business critical, Business operational, Administrative);\n  - `technicalFit` and `functionalFit` (Excellent, Adequate, Insufficient, Unreasonable);\n  - `annualCost` and `monthlyCost` (numbers);\n  - `dataClassification` (public, internal, confidential, restricted);\n  - `external` (true for systems run by another organisation);\n  - `admPhase`, a TOGAF ADM phase such as `\"C: Information Systems Architectures\"`.\n- **C4:** `technology` and `location` (Internal or External). `kind: Database` or `kind: Queue` on a\n  container changes its shape.\n- **ArchiMate:** `domain` (Business, Application, Technology or Implementation) on common-domain\n  elements such as `archimate:Service`, `archimate:Process` and `archimate:Function`.\n- **DDD:**\n  - `subdomainType` (Core, Supporting, Generic) on `ddd:Subdomain`;\n  - on a bounded context or software system, the canvas fields: `purpose`, `domainClassification`,\n    `businessModel`, `evolution`, `ubiquitousLanguage` and `businessDecisions`.\n\n## relationships\n\n| Key | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `from`, `to` | yes | Element names. `from` depends on or acts on `to`. |\n| `type` | yes | A relationship type id, for example `c4:Uses`, `archimate:Serving`, `archimate:Realization`, `archimate:Triggering`, `ddd:CustomerSupplier` or `togaf:Realizes`. |\n| `label` | no | Verb phrase, for example \"Places orders via\". |\n| `technology` | no | Protocol or format, for example \"JSON/HTTPS\" or \"Kafka\". |\n| `properties` | no | For example `accessType: Read` on `archimate:Access`, `downstreamRoles: ACL` on DDD patterns, or `dataFlow: none` where no data passes. |\n\nA relationship is matched by `from`, `to` and `type`. Applying it again updates the label.\n\nNotation conventions:\n- **C4:** `c4:Uses` between people, systems, containers and components.\n- **ArchiMate:**\n  - a system realises a capability: `archimate:Realization` from system to capability;\n  - a service serves a process or actor: `archimate:Serving`;\n  - process steps trigger each other: `archimate:Triggering`;\n  - value stream stages flow: `archimate:Flow`;\n  - a value stream is made of stages: `archimate:Composition`;\n  - a driver influences a goal or requirement: `archimate:Influence`;\n  - a system realises a requirement: `archimate:Realization`.\n- **DDD:** `ddd:Implements` (system → subdomain), `ddd:Owns` (team → system), context-map patterns\n  between systems (`ddd:CustomerSupplier`, `ddd:Conformist`, `ddd:AnticorruptionLayer`,\n  `ddd:OpenHostService`, `ddd:PublishedLanguage`, `ddd:Partnership`, `ddd:SharedKernel`,\n  `ddd:SeparateWays`), and `ddd:Triggers` between event-storm stickies.\n- **TOGAF:** `togaf:Realizes` (solution building block → architecture building block), `togaf:Uses`.\n\n## views\n\n| Key | Required | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `name` | yes | View name. An existing view with this name is updated. |\n| `notation` | for new views, unless `viewpoint` is given | `c4`, `archimate`, `ddd`, `togaf` or `generic`. |\n| `viewpoint` | recommended | A viewpoint id, for example `c4:container` or `archimate:capability-map`. |\n| `subject` | for focused views | The element the view is about (C4 context, container or component; DDD canvas). |\n| `rows` | one of `rows` or `elements` | Element names in rows, top to bottom: `[[A, B], [C]]`. |\n| `elements` | | Element names; laid out four per row. |\n| `alsoShow` | no | Other notations whose relationships to draw, for example `[c4]` on an ArchiMate view. |\n| `description`, `properties` | no | For example `properties: { admPhase: \"B: Business Architecture\" }`. |\n\nChildren listed together with their parent are drawn inside it, so you can list a system and its\ncontainers in one row. Relationships of the view's notation between listed elements are drawn\nautomatically. A view listed again with the same elements keeps its manual layout from the app.\n\n`ddd:canvas` views need only `subject`. The canvas reads the subject's properties.\n\n## decisions\n\n```yaml\ndecisions:\n  - title: Use an event bus between ordering and fulfilment\n    status: accepted        # proposed, accepted, deprecated, superseded\n    date: 2026-02-12\n    elements: [Order API, Event Bus]\n    body: |\n      ## Context\n      ...\n      ## Decision\n      ...\n      ## Consequences\n      ...\n```\n\nA decision with the same title (or `id`) is updated. New decisions are numbered after the highest\nexisting number.\n\n## rules\n\nArchitecture rules are added to `vastitecture.yaml` and replaced by `id`. Examples:\n\n```yaml\nrules:\n  - id: systems-have-owners\n    description: Every system has an owner.\n    severity: warning\n    match: { type: c4:SoftwareSystem }\n    require: [owner, lifecycle]\n  - id: no-new-legacy-dependencies\n    forbid: { from: { lifecycle: [plan, phaseIn] }, to: { tag: legacy } }\n```\n\n## remove\n\n```yaml\nremove:\n  elements: [Old CRM]\n  relationships: [{ from: Web App, to: Old CRM, type: c4:Uses }]\n  views: [Old landscape]\n  decisions: [\"0003\"]\n```\n\nRemovals happen before additions, so a spec can replace something in one go.\n"
};
